# 4. Closest Login Pair — Corrected and Optimized Pseudocode

## A. Corrected Algorithm: Brute-Force Closest Pair

**Input:** List of login coordinates.

**Output:** Indices of the closest pair and their Euclidean distance.

```text
ALGORITHM CORRECTED_CLOSEST_LOGINS(events)

    IF LENGTH(events) < 2
        RETURN no pair
    END IF

    minimum_distance ← INFINITY
    closest_pair ← none

    FOR i ← 0 TO LENGTH(events) - 2
        FOR j ← i + 1 TO LENGTH(events) - 1

            distance ← EUCLIDEAN_DISTANCE(events[i], events[j])

            IF distance < minimum_distance
                minimum_distance ← distance
                closest_pair ← (i, j)
            END IF

        END FOR
    END FOR

    RETURN closest_pair, minimum_distance

END ALGORITHM
```

## B. Optimized Algorithm: Sorting and Pruning

```text
ALGORITHM OPTIMIZED_CLOSEST_LOGINS(events)

    IF LENGTH(events) < 2
        RETURN no pair
    END IF

    SORT points by x-coordinate
    RETAIN each point's original index

    best_squared ← INFINITY
    closest_pair ← none

    FOR i ← 0 TO LENGTH(points) - 2

        FOR j ← i + 1 TO LENGTH(points) - 1

            dx ← points[j].x - points[i].x

            IF dx * dx >= best_squared
                BREAK
            END IF

            dy ← points[j].y - points[i].y
            distance_squared ← dx * dx + dy * dy

            IF distance_squared < best_squared
                best_squared ← distance_squared
                closest_pair ← original indices of points i and j
            END IF

        END FOR
    END FOR

    RETURN closest_pair, SQRT(best_squared)

END ALGORITHM
```

**Time Complexity:**
- Corrected brute-force algorithm: O(n²).
- Optimized sorting and pruning: O(n log n) for sorting, but the complete algorithm still has O(n²) worst-case time complexity.

**Purpose:** Identify the two login events with the smallest Euclidean distance. Sorting and pruning can reduce comparisons on many inputs, while preserving the original indices of the events.
